1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,667
Every real number has a
home on the number line.

2
00:00:03,667 --> 00:00:05,192
Minus two lives here.

3
00:00:05,192 --> 00:00:06,509
Three lives there.

4
00:00:06,909 --> 00:00:09,216
But where does three plus two i live?

5
00:00:09,216 --> 00:00:11,478
Not left of zero, not right of it.

6
00:00:11,478 --> 00:00:13,087
The line is already full.

7
00:00:13,537 --> 00:00:16,845
Diya found the answer as she
walked into college today.

8
00:00:17,195 --> 00:00:20,903
In Bengaluru, an address can
be fourth main, second cross.

9
00:00:20,903 --> 00:00:22,537
Two numbers, one house.

10
00:00:22,937 --> 00:00:25,703
Complex numbers get the
same kind of address.

11
00:00:25,703 --> 00:00:28,423
Today we draw their map: the Argand plane.

12
00:00:29,073 --> 00:00:30,435
Here is the idea.

13
00:00:30,785 --> 00:00:33,920
Keep the real number line,
flat, from left to right.

14
00:00:33,920 --> 00:00:37,098
Then add a second line through
zero, going straight up.

15
00:00:37,498 --> 00:00:39,924
The flat line is the real axis.

16
00:00:39,924 --> 00:00:42,788
The upright line is the imaginary axis.

17
00:00:43,188 --> 00:00:47,715
Now the complex number x plus
i y sits at the point x, y.

18
00:00:47,715 --> 00:00:49,744
Go x along, then y up.

19
00:00:50,344 --> 00:00:55,013
Every complex number gets exactly one
point, and every point gets exactly

20
00:00:55,013 --> 00:00:56,438
one complex number.

21
00:00:56,438 --> 00:00:59,785
This is the complex plane,
or the Argand plane.

22
00:01:00,385 --> 00:01:05,309
It is named after Jean Robert Argand,
who described it in an essay in eighteen

23
00:01:05,309 --> 00:01:06,209
oh six.

24
00:01:06,025 --> 00:01:10,263
Caspar Wessel had published the
same idea in seventeen ninety nine.

25
00:01:10,863 --> 00:01:14,262
Watch the map fill up, with
points from the NCERT figure.

26
00:01:14,612 --> 00:01:15,917
Two plus four i.

27
00:01:15,917 --> 00:01:17,474
Two along, four up.

28
00:01:17,874 --> 00:01:19,467
Minus two plus three i.

29
00:01:19,467 --> 00:01:21,349
Two to the left, three up.

30
00:01:21,749 --> 00:01:23,599
Minus five minus two i.

31
00:01:23,599 --> 00:01:25,329
Five left, two down.

32
00:01:25,329 --> 00:01:26,732
One minus two i.

33
00:01:26,732 --> 00:01:28,462
One right, two down.

34
00:01:28,862 --> 00:01:32,023
Two plus zero i lands on the real axis.

35
00:01:32,023 --> 00:01:35,471
Zero plus one i lands
on the imaginary axis.

36
00:01:35,471 --> 00:01:37,432
That point is i itself.

37
00:01:37,832 --> 00:01:43,147
So real numbers live on the real axis,
and purely imaginary numbers live

38
00:01:43,147 --> 00:01:45,012
on the imaginary axis.

39
00:01:45,662 --> 00:01:46,562
Quick one.

40
00:01:46,507 --> 00:01:48,508
Where does minus four i live?

41
00:01:48,508 --> 00:01:49,702
Pause and think.

42
00:01:52,902 --> 00:01:56,070
On the imaginary axis,
four steps below zero.

43
00:01:56,070 --> 00:02:00,204
Its real part is zero, so it
never leaves the upright line.

44
00:02:00,854 --> 00:02:03,676
The map also shows two
old friends for free.

45
00:02:04,026 --> 00:02:08,802
The distance from O to the point is
the modulus: root of x squared plus

46
00:02:08,802 --> 00:02:09,705
y squared.

47
00:02:10,105 --> 00:02:15,012
And the conjugate, x minus i y, is
the mirror image of the point in the

48
00:02:15,012 --> 00:02:15,912
real axis.

49
00:02:16,509 --> 00:02:20,104
Now the map's superpower: the
distance between two numbers.

50
00:02:20,454 --> 00:02:21,441
Step one.

51
00:02:21,441 --> 00:02:22,355
Subtract.

52
00:02:22,355 --> 00:02:28,422
z one minus z two is x one minus x
two, plus i times y one minus y two.

53
00:02:29,172 --> 00:02:30,072
Step two.

54
00:02:29,988 --> 00:02:34,437
Its modulus is the root of those
two differences, squared and added.

55
00:02:35,187 --> 00:02:36,099
Step three.

56
00:02:36,099 --> 00:02:39,919
That is exactly the distance
formula between the two points.

57
00:02:40,669 --> 00:02:45,597
So the modulus of z one minus z two
is the distance between them on the

58
00:02:45,597 --> 00:02:46,497
map.

59
00:02:46,798 --> 00:02:48,869
Now watch a rule draw a shape.

60
00:02:48,869 --> 00:02:52,087
Fix the real part of z
at two, and let z move.

61
00:02:52,087 --> 00:02:54,292
It traces a straight upright line.

62
00:02:54,692 --> 00:02:57,665
Now fix the modulus of z at two instead.

63
00:02:57,665 --> 00:02:59,870
z must stay two away from O.

64
00:02:59,870 --> 00:03:01,404
It traces a circle.

65
00:03:02,004 --> 00:03:03,140
Side by side.

66
00:03:03,140 --> 00:03:05,412
Real part equals two: a line.

67
00:03:05,412 --> 00:03:07,621
Modulus equals two: a circle.

68
00:03:07,621 --> 00:03:10,744
One small change, a
totally different shape.

69
00:03:11,144 --> 00:03:12,263
Now you try.

70
00:03:12,263 --> 00:03:15,228
The modulus of z minus one equals three.

71
00:03:15,228 --> 00:03:17,019
A line, or a circle?

72
00:03:17,019 --> 00:03:18,410
Pause and decide.

73
00:03:21,610 --> 00:03:22,510
A circle!

74
00:03:22,450 --> 00:03:25,681
Every such z is three
away from the point one.

75
00:03:25,681 --> 00:03:27,567
Centre one, radius three.

76
00:03:28,217 --> 00:03:29,282
Remember this.

77
00:03:29,282 --> 00:03:31,970
x plus i y lives at the point x, y.

78
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:35,614
And distance on the map is
the modulus of a difference.

79
00:03:36,314 --> 00:03:38,049
Now, one worked example.

80
00:03:38,399 --> 00:03:43,166
Find the distance between z one, one
plus two i, and z two, four minus

81
00:03:43,166 --> 00:03:44,066
two i.

82
00:03:44,339 --> 00:03:45,239
Step one.

83
00:03:45,195 --> 00:03:48,496
z one minus z two is
minus three plus four i.

84
00:03:49,246 --> 00:03:50,149
Step two.

85
00:03:50,149 --> 00:03:53,496
Its modulus is the root
of nine plus sixteen.

86
00:03:54,246 --> 00:03:55,210
Step three.

87
00:03:55,210 --> 00:03:57,098
Root twenty five is five.

88
00:03:57,848 --> 00:04:00,320
So the two points are five units apart.

89
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:03,641
On the map, that is a
three, four, five triangle.

90
00:04:04,291 --> 00:04:05,757
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:06,107 --> 00:04:09,021
The modulus of z plus two i equals three.

92
00:04:09,021 --> 00:04:12,038
Many students put the
centre at plus two i.

93
00:04:12,438 --> 00:04:15,662
But z plus two i is z minus, minus two i.

94
00:04:15,662 --> 00:04:19,255
So the centre is minus
two i, two steps below O.

95
00:04:19,255 --> 00:04:20,825
The radius is three.

96
00:04:21,475 --> 00:04:22,918
Where is this asked?

97
00:04:22,918 --> 00:04:26,974
In JEE Main, as equations in z
that draw a line or a circle.

98
00:04:27,574 --> 00:04:28,812
Exam level now.

99
00:04:28,812 --> 00:04:31,572
On which line do all these points lie?

100
00:04:31,572 --> 00:04:32,863
Pause and solve.

101
00:04:38,263 --> 00:04:39,515
The answer is A.

102
00:04:39,515 --> 00:04:41,972
x plus two y plus three equals zero.

103
00:04:42,472 --> 00:04:44,238
Let z be x plus i y.

104
00:04:44,238 --> 00:04:49,072
The left side, squared, is x minus
two, squared, plus y squared.

105
00:04:49,572 --> 00:04:53,893
The right side, squared, is x
squared, plus y plus four, squared.

106
00:04:54,393 --> 00:04:56,772
Expand, and the squares cancel.

107
00:04:56,772 --> 00:05:00,450
Minus four x plus four
equals eight y plus sixteen.

108
00:05:00,450 --> 00:05:03,198
So x plus two y plus three is zero.

109
00:05:03,798 --> 00:05:05,256
Here is your recap.

110
00:05:05,256 --> 00:05:06,756
Screenshot this card.

111
00:05:09,956 --> 00:05:12,954
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

112
00:05:13,354 --> 00:05:14,254
One.

113
00:05:13,909 --> 00:05:15,813
Plot minus three plus i.

114
00:05:15,813 --> 00:05:17,716
Which quadrant is it in?

115
00:05:18,616 --> 00:05:19,516
Two.

116
00:05:19,156 --> 00:05:22,140
Where do the purely
imaginary numbers live?

117
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:23,940
Three.

118
00:05:23,693 --> 00:05:27,278
What shape does the modulus
of z equals four draw?

119
00:05:28,378 --> 00:05:32,550
Next time: what if every address
were a distance and an angle, instead

120
00:05:32,550 --> 00:05:33,739
of along and up?

121
00:05:33,739 --> 00:05:35,304
That is the polar form.
